+QtXhRiEJ9zo,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Once You Hit $100K The Math Does The Rest,7837,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::090_QtXhRiEJ9zo_Once_You_Hit_100K_The_Math_Does_The_Rest.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""6f15d49f002e5e7d96f780075ca83e74bc80f131699a89d077c9be6884014ed5"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""QtXhRiEJ9zo"", ""Words"": ""3101""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QtXhRiEJ9zo"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QtXhRiEJ9zo"", ""audit-batches/NICK_150_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The full 17,218-character canonical stored caption text was read in three contiguous ranges (0-6,000, 6,000-12,000, 12,000-17,218). The opening uses a lunch-break account surprise ($1,000 contributed, $2,300 growth) to dramatize the market out-earning the saver, then withholds the balance that would make that monthly gain plausible. Marcus supplies the main narrative spine: $1,000 monthly into an index fund, discouraging early progress, milestone acceleration and an eventual $1 million. Dana is a counterfactual foil who cashes out $40,000 for consumption; her 37-year opportunity cost supplies emotional stakes. The Munger anecdote transitions into a clear two-engine explanation—contributions and market returns—and the annual-contribution/expected-return formula is the strongest educational beat because it reveals that the crossover moves with behavior and assumptions. A milestone ladder then makes acceleration concrete. The script broadens into no-more-contributions examples, delay costs and rule-of-72 shorthand, before a creator-community promotion interrupts the financial argument. The second half introduces limitations through Federal Reserve, Vanguard, saving-rate and historical-return claims, then returns to inflation, lifestyle interruption, fees and Buffett longevity. That caveat section is structurally useful, but several statistics mix different populations or dates and the repeated motivational language ('mathematical threshold', 'the math hands one person') risks making assumed returns feel deterministic. The final recap accurately says the first $100K is mostly contributions but again says the market 'takes over' without restating the $120K-$170K crossover. A duplicated creator-community closing line appears in the stored captions. The closing financial-advice disclaimer does not repair omitted tax, fee, inflation, return-sequence or contribution-timing assumptions. Stored captions only; spoken wording, visuals, music, edits, retention and sponsor disclosures were not verified.","[{""beat"": ""Lunch-break surprise hook; six slow years; exact-$100K promise; Nick intro and like request"", ""character_range"": [0, 1030]}, {""beat"": ""Marcus starts $1,000 monthly; first-year $12,400; flat-part frustration and behavioral quitting pressure"", ""character_range"": [1030, 3234]}, {""beat"": ""Years two to four; Dana cashes out $40,000; SUV/kitchen opportunity-cost story; Munger quotation"", ""character_range"": [3234, 5200]}, {""beat"": ""Two engines explanation; annual contribution divided by expected return; $170K at 7% and $120K at 10%"", ""character_range"": [5200, 6938]}, {""beat"": ""Marcus milestone ladder from first $100K to $1M; 7% and 10% variants"", ""character_range"": [6938, 8204]}, {""beat"": ""Stop-contributing examples; age 30 to 65; rule of 72; age-25 versus age-35 delay cost"", ""character_range"": [8204, 9680]}, {""beat"": ""Creator-community interruption; Fed/Vanguard retirement-savings statistics; saving-rate and household-income path"", ""character_range"": [9680, 11314]}, {""beat"": ""Historical-return volatility; negative years and lost decade; Munger $100K inflation adjustment"", ""character_range"": [11314, 12977]}, {""beat"": ""Lifestyle interruption; future-dollar framing; pre-$100K savings-rate priority; automation, broad index funds and fee warning"", ""character_range"": [12977, 15135]}, {""beat"": ""Buffett longevity statistic and snowball quotation; three-sentence recap and loading metaphor"", ""character_range"": [15135, 16460]}, {""beat"": ""Repeated creator-school promotion and educational-purpose disclaimer"", ""character_range"": [16460, 17218]}]","[""Core crossover formula is correct as an expected-return identity: balance = annual contribution / assumed annual return. With $12,000 annual contributions, the script's approximately $171,000 at 7% and $120,000 at 10% reproduce. The title's exact $100K switch does not: at $100,000, modeled annual gains are $7,000 or $10,000, both below $12,000 contributed. Returns are volatile and not a salary; the equality is an expectation under a stated rate, not a guaranteed date."", ""Marcus milestone ladder substantially reproduces under end-of-month $1,000 deposits and nominal annual rates compounded monthly. At 7%: first $100K about 6.67 years; each next $100K about 4.42, 3.42, 2.75, 2.33, 2.00, 1.75, 1.58, 1.42 and 1.25 years. At 10%: first $100K about 6.17 years and $900K to $1M about 1.0 year. Results depend on deposit timing, compounding convention, uninterrupted contributions, taxes, fees and realized return path, none fully specified."", ""Dana's $40,000 at age 28 becoming roughly $1.3M by 65 is arithmetically plausible at a fixed 10% annual return: about $1.36M over 37 years. It is a nominal, pre-tax, pre-fee deterministic illustration; the SUV and renovation amounts do not establish that all $40,000 would otherwise remain invested."", ""$100,000 from age 30 to 65 becomes about $1.068M at 7% or $2.810M at 10%; $100,000 from age 25 to 65 becomes about $4.526M at 10%, versus $1.745M from age 35, a gap of about $2.781M. Those arithmetic examples reproduce with annual compounding but are nominal endpoint scenarios, not forecasts. The rule of 72 is an approximation and does not supply taxes, inflation, fees or volatility."", ""Federal Reserve SCF 2022 says 54.3% of families held retirement accounts, defined there as IRAs, Keoghs and certain employer-sponsored accounts. Its 45.7% complement means no account in that SCF category, not necessarily zero assets intended for retirement. Federal Reserve SHED 2022 separately reports 28% of non-retired adults had no retirement savings and notes 47% had retirement savings outside formal retirement accounts. The script's adjacent 'Roughly 46% have zero' wording is therefore ambiguous and overbroad unless explicitly limited to SCF-defined account ownership."", ""Vanguard How America Saves 2025 reports 2024 average $148,153 and median $38,176 for Vanguard defined-contribution-plan participants; the rounded numbers and skew explanation match. Vanguard cautions that current DC balances are only a partial measure of retirement preparedness, and about 75% of participants were below the average. These are not balances for all Americans, all households or all retirement assets."", ""The 2024 Census median household income available before publication was $83,730, supporting 'about $84,000'. BEA's latest available personal-saving-rate release before the 2026-06-09 upload reported 2.6% for April 2026, not 3.5%; March was 3.6%. The script may be using an older/revised month without naming it. At $84,000 income and 3.5%, $245 monthly reaches $100K in about 19 years only around a 6% nominal annual return; the rate, compounding, tax and contribution timing are omitted. With no return it takes about 34 years."", ""NYU professor Aswath Damodaran's 1928-2025 total-return series reports an 11.85% arithmetic average and 26 negative years out of 98, supporting the script's broad 'around 10%' and 'one out of every four years negative' statements. An arithmetic average is not the same as compounded investor return. The script's exact 'five years between 8 and 12%' count and 2000-2009 'basically nothing' statement were not independently reproduced from a frozen primary table in this review; retain as UNVERIFIED rather than treating them as established."", ""The $100,000 mid-1990s to about $211,000 current-dollar comparison is sensitive to the chosen starting month/year and the current CPI month. BLS confirms its calculator uses calendar-year average CPI and the latest monthly index for the current year, but the script gives only 'mid-90s' and no calculation date; the exact $211,000 is not reproducible from the stated inputs and remains PARTIAL."", ""The 1% advisory-fee warning is directionally and conditionally plausible. SEC Investor.gov shows that ongoing fees reduce the balance left to compound. Under one transparent 35-year illustration, reducing a fixed 7% gross return to 6% lowers terminal wealth by about 28%, close to 'around a quarter'. The fraction changes with horizon, contributions, gross return and fee application, so it is not universal."", ""The Buffett '3% by age 65 / 97% after' statistic relies on age-specific net-worth estimates and changing Berkshire/share values; no primary Berkshire source for that exact decomposition was found in this review. The conclusion that long compounding time mattered is plausible, but the exact percentages and 'other fund managers have tripled his percentage' are UNSOURCED here.""]","Somewhere out there, right now, someone is checking their investment account on their lunch break, and they're about to notice something strange. Last month, they put in $1,000, same as always. But, their account grew by 2,300.","YouTube is honestly one of the best opportunities of 2026 if it's done right, and the door's open. Link is in the description. I'm waiting for you. Just a reminder, I'm not a financial adviser. This video is for educational purposes only and any results depend on your own decisions and actions.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio and editing unverified,,False,,,,,
+QtlJUTJ58ZU,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Do the Opposite: The Wealth Trick Rich People Use (Inversion Thinking),19992,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::260_QtlJUTJ58ZU_Do_the_Opposite_The_Wealth_Trick_Rich_People_Use_Inversion_Thinking.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""8ac56cea13feaf07276bc5beb6ca0edffe7172b251d7d0d6ab9f3e9784087800"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""QtlJUTJ58ZU"", ""Words"": ""3398""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QtlJUTJ58ZU"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QtlJUTJ58ZU"", ""audit-batches/NICK_151_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The full 21,036-character canonical stored caption text was read in four contiguous ranges (0-6,000, 6,000-12,000, 12,000-18,000 and 18,000-21,036). The hook contrasts fashionable tactics with Munger/Buffett and reframes 'get rich' as 'do not stay poor'. A subscribe/like CTA arrives before the core teaching. The first act explains inversion through Jacobi and Munger, then uses a leaking-bucket analogy to move from principle to personal finance. Threat-avoidance and lottery-winner claims try to provide a psychological rationale. A Bezos regret-minimization story broadens the model from avoiding mistakes to avoiding future regret. The central payoff is an eleven-item anti-checklist: blaming income, revolving credit, lifestyle inflation, status purchases, no emergency fund, financial ignorance, perfectionist delay, unnecessary complexity, emotional decisions, harmful social norms, and extracting home equity for consumption. Repeated ordinal transitions make the long list easy to follow, but only the first ten receive full development; item eleven is introduced and then the script jumps immediately to the conclusion. The closing compresses the thesis into 'stop being stupid with money' and compound interest, without a final operational worksheet or prioritization. The strongest actionable material is the explicit failure-mode list. The weakest reasoning universalizes anecdotes and moralizes structural or emergency outcomes: external shocks are called excuses, income is said to have 'nothing' to do with wealth outcomes, lottery failure is asserted without a cited study, and social-circle prediction is presented with 'scary accuracy' but no measurement. Captions end abruptly after the conclusion; no spoken/audio completeness or editing claim is made.","[{""beat"": ""Contrarian investor hook; Munger/Buffett authority; inversion promise; early subscribe/like CTA"", ""character_range"": [0, 1306]}, {""beat"": ""Positive advice versus avoiding negative outcomes; Jacobi/Munger origin story; Berkshire and mistake-avoidance framing"", ""character_range"": [1306, 3759]}, {""beat"": ""Leaking-bucket analogy; cross-income universality claim; threat-avoidance rationale; lottery-winner example"", ""character_range"": [3759, 6124]}, {""beat"": ""Bezos regret-minimization story; future regret; immediate-results promise; transition to failure modes"", ""character_range"": [6124, 8150]}, {""beat"": ""Failure mode 1: treating income as the problem; teacher/doctor contrast; responsibility framing"", ""character_range"": [8150, 9569]}, {""beat"": ""Failure mode 2: credit cards, high interest and weakened payment salience"", ""character_range"": [9569, 10827]}, {""beat"": ""Failure mode 3: lifestyle inflation and rising fixed costs"", ""character_range"": [10827, 11876]}, {""beat"": ""Failure modes 4-5: status consumption, $500 car-payment opportunity cost, emergency fund and forced liquidation"", ""character_range"": [11876, 13826]}, {""beat"": ""Failure modes 6-7: financial ignorance and perfectionist delay; $200 early-investor illustration"", ""character_range"": [13826, 16330]}, {""beat"": ""Failure mode 8: complexity trap; simple index/automation alternative; attributed Munger lesson"", ""character_range"": [16330, 17735]}, {""beat"": ""Failure modes 9-10: emotional decisions and contagious social norms"", ""character_range"": [17735, 19934]}, {""beat"": ""Failure mode 11: extracting home equity for consumption; immediate recap and compound-interest close"", ""character_range"": [19934, 21036]}]","[""SUPPORTED: Charlie Munger's 2007 USC address explicitly says problems often become easier when turned around, illustrates asking what does the worst damage and how to avoid it, and recommends asking what will fail. This supports the core inversion method. It does not establish that inversion itself created more millionaires than any other framework, that rich people generally use it, or that avoiding listed mistakes makes wealth inevitable."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: The script attributes 'invert, always invert' and the origin of the method to mathematician Carl Jacobi. Munger repeatedly popularized inversion and the Jacobi maxim is widely attributed, but the reviewed Munger speech transcript establishes inversion's use rather than a primary Jacobi document. The origin/wording remains secondary-source provenance in this audit."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: Berkshire's 2024 annual letter reports a 19.9% compounded annual gain in per-share market value for 1965-2024 versus 10.4% for the S&P 500 with dividends. That supports exceptional historical shareholder returns. It does not isolate 'simply avoiding mistakes' as the cause, and the script's 'thousands of shareholders wealthy' is not quantified."", ""UNVERIFIED: 'This one shift in perspective has created more millionaires than any other mental framework' is a comparative causal claim with no population, outcome definition or source in the stored text. 'Building wealth becomes almost inevitable' is likewise too strong; income, expenses, shocks, taxes, investment choice and returns still matter."", ""UNVERIFIED: 'Most lottery winners end up broke within a few years' is presented without a named dataset or study. No lottery population, jurisdiction, time horizon or definition of broke is supplied, so it is not used as evidence for inversion."", ""MISLEADING: 'The difference has nothing to do with how much money comes in' overstates the teacher/doctor anecdote. Spending, debt and behavior matter, but income directly affects feasible saving capacity. The examples are unsourced and cannot establish income irrelevance. The adjacent claim that every broke person has an excuse also collapses job loss, health, divorce and macro shocks into personal failure."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: The script's '20% annual interest' credit-card warning is realistic. The Federal Reserve's November 2025 report states average rates around 21.5% across all accounts and about 22.8% for accounts assessed interest at end-2024. But people who pay within the grace period do not incur finance charges, so card use and revolving high-interest debt should be distinguished."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: Prelec and Simester found higher willingness-to-pay in a credit-card condition for uncertain-value auction goods, but the effect was not always present; the second study's overall payment-method main effect was not significant and context mattered. The script's mechanistic statement that swiping removes pain and physical cash creates loss aversion is broader and more certain than this evidence."", ""SUPPORTED_WITH_ASSUMPTIONS: Investing $500 at each month-end for 30 years grows to about $609,986 at a nominal 7% annual rate compounded monthly, so 'over $600,000' reproduces near 7%. It is $180,000 with no return and about $745,180 at 8%. The script omits return, contribution timing, taxes, fees, inflation, volatility and interruption; it is a scenario, not the cost of every $500 car payment."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: IRS guidance confirms early retirement-plan or IRA withdrawals can be taxable and generally can face an additional 10% tax before age 59.5, subject to material exceptions; hardship distributions permanently reduce the plan balance. The script says one 'might' pay penalties and taxes, which is directionally sound, but account type, age and exception determine treatment."", ""SUPPORTED_WITH_ASSUMPTIONS: A person contributing $200 at each month-end from age 25 through 34 and then stopping can have more at 65 than someone contributing $200 monthly from 35 through 64, but only above roughly a 6.11% nominal annual return compounded monthly. At 7%, the modeled endpoints are about $280,968 versus $243,994; at 4%, the later saver has more ($138,810 versus $97,582). The script gives no return, fees, taxes, inflation or deposit-timing assumption, so presenting the outcome as automatic is misleading."", ""UNVERIFIED: 'Show me someone's five closest friends, and I can predict their financial future with scary accuracy' is not accompanied by a predictive study, measurement rule or accuracy rate. Munger's discussion of social-proof tendency supports the general possibility that peer behavior influences choices, not this quantitative-sounding prediction."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: The last item warns against repeatedly extracting home equity for consumption. That identifies leverage and housing-security risks, but the stored captions provide no rates, terms, loan-to-value, tax treatment or distinction between consumption, consolidation and productive uses. The abrupt transition prevents a full mechanism explanation.""]","Have you ever noticed that the smartest investors in the world seem to make money by doing the exact opposite of what everyone else is doing? While most people are chasing the latest get-rich-quick scheme or trying to time the perfect market entry, billionaires like","wealth, you can build a fortune by simply not sabotaging yourself. Charlie Munger became a billionaire not by being the smartest guy in the room, but by consistently avoiding the obvious mistakes that destroy everyone else. Start there and let compound interest handle the rest.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timing and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+R1AcuUo916k,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Private Credit Panic - Why Investors Are Rushing For the Exits,4790,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::019_R1AcuUo916k_Private_Credit_Panic_Why_Investors_Are_Rushing_For_the_Exits.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""86dedc55cbaeb9a31458d88c5586ee0c283953da2d56c0f5b0eb3a11e9723705"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""R1AcuUo916k"", ""Words"": ""3460""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:R1AcuUo916k"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:R1AcuUo916k"", ""audit-batches/NICK_152_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The script uses a named-but-unsourced retiree story to turn illiquidity into a concrete household problem, then repeatedly returns to a clean organizing idea: every asset has an appraised price and an executable price. It explains direct lending, interval/non-traded BDC repurchase mechanics, valuation lag and borrower stress through analogies before offering three filing checks. The narrative escalates from individual redemption to issuer failures, industry queues and retirement-plan policy, inserts a community CTA, then steelmans the opposing case: locked capital can prevent forced selling and patient holders may recover. The final advice is narrower and calmer than the title/thumbnail. Strengths are structure, counterargument and actionable document terms. Weaknesses are composite or unverifiable characters, unsupported universal statements, conflation of distinct private-credit vehicles, and several statistics whose populations and dates are omitted. The simple claim that a 5% quarterly cap means five years assumes 20 equal quarterly payments against the original balance; actual percentage-of-outstanding mechanics, proration, subscriptions, distributions, NAV changes and board discretion alter timing.","[{""beat"": ""Alvin's partial-redemption cold open; private-credit scale and relevance; early subscribe CTA"", ""character_range"": [0, 1690]}, {""beat"": ""Two-prices thesis; post-2008 bank-to-fund migration; corporate lending mechanics"", ""character_range"": [1690, 4490]}, {""beat"": ""First Brands and Tricolor failures; Dimon cockroach quote; uncertainty triggers exits"", ""character_range"": [4490, 6080]}, {""beat"": ""Retail fund quarterly repurchases, 5% cap, proration and five-year simple exit arithmetic"", ""character_range"": [6080, 8350]}, {""beat"": ""Blue Owl valuation episode; public BDC discounts; volatility-laundering analogy"", ""character_range"": [8350, 11060]}, {""beat"": ""Investor liquidity consequences; three diagnostic tells: PIK income, nonaccruals and extend-and-pretend"", ""character_range"": [11060, 13330]}, {""beat"": ""Fitch default comparison; 401(k) policy expansion; fees and five-year return comparison"", ""character_range"": [13330, 15160]}, {""beat"": ""Strongest counterargument: long lockups, gates, loss avoidance and BREIT precedent"", ""character_range"": [15160, 16940]}, {""beat"": ""Bank financing linkage; non-blaming synthesis; repurchase-term checklist and two-prices close"", ""character_range"": [16940, 18792]}]","[""SUPPORTED: Executive Order 14330, dated 2025-08-07, directed Labor to reconsider guidance and potentially create safe harbors for asset-allocation funds containing alternative assets, including private debt. DOL's 2026-03-30 proposal establishes process-based safe harbors. The script's policy sequence is directionally accurate, but inclusion in any particular target-date plan is not automatic."", ""SUPPORTED: Blue Owl Technology Income Corp.'s SEC-filed Q2 2026 FAQ reports requests equal to 38.1% of shares and a quarterly tender capacity of 5%; its filed offer states oversubscriptions may be prorated. A Blue Owl Alternative Credit Fund filing likewise states unfilled requests receive no priority and must be resubmitted. These primary filings support the queue mechanism, not the script's anonymous Alvin chronology."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: The transcript says a 5% quarterly cap implies five years to exit. Twenty quarters equals five years if exactly 5% of the original position is paid each quarter. Fund offers are generally percentages of outstanding shares and may be prorated; NAV, new subscriptions and offer size can change, so five years is an illustration rather than a guaranteed wait."", ""SUPPORTED_AS_ALLEGATION: The SEC's 2026 Tricolor case alleges more than $1.9 billion raised through ABS and double-pledging of loans. Jefferies' August 2026 filing says First Brands proceedings allege duplicate, inflated and fabricated receivables and values its related investment at zero. These are allegations/proceedings, not final adjudications of every figure in the script."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: Federal Reserve research documents rapid growth of bank lending to private-credit vehicles and identifies limited visibility and potential financial-stability channels. The exact script statement that this is 13% of all bank lending versus 4% in 2015 was not reproduced from the reviewed accessible data and remains unverified as phrased."", ""UNVERIFIED: The opening Alvin, Erica and Karina stories have no named fund, filing, interview or source in the stored captions. They function as narrative illustrations and cannot be treated as observed cases."", ""UNVERIFIED: The aggregate claims of $15 billion requested, 53% returned across the 12 largest funds, average requests of 12%, 8% of interest income as PIK, 2.8% nonaccruals, and the exact Fitch over-9% comparison lack named tables and measurement definitions in the transcript. They may be plausible but are not independently certified here."", ""MISLEADING: 'outside anybody's ability to tell you what they are worth' overstates valuation uncertainty. Private loans lack continuous exchange prices and estimates can be stale or model-dependent, but managers, auditors and third-party valuation processes do estimate fair value under accounting rules."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: The 5% tender structure and public-market discounts demonstrate a liquidity/valuation distinction. A public BDC's discount to NAV is not automatically proof that every underlying loan is overvalued; discounts can also reflect fees, leverage, expected losses, governance, taxes, sentiment and liquidity."", ""UNVERIFIED: The five-year $100,000 comparisons ($181,000 total U.S. stock market, $156,000 private credit, $122,000 high yield) omit exact tickers/indexes, start/end dates, reinvestment, fees and tax assumptions, so they are not reproducible from the stored text."", ""SUPPORTED_WITH_QUALIFICATION: DOL's proposal explicitly requires fiduciaries to consider performance, fees, liquidity, valuation, benchmarks and complexity. This supports the video's relevance to retirement savers while cutting against the implication that alternatives can be inserted without fiduciary review.""]","Your account balance has gone up every single quarter for 11 years. Never a red month, never a dip, just a smooth, polite staircase climbing up and to the right, which should probably have been your first clue.","So, the next time somebody shows you an investment with a line that never goes down, do not ask what it earned, ask what somebody would hand you for it this afternoon. If they cannot tell you, you already have your answer.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timing and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+RUVOjfrIemY,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Why Your Credit Score Drops Even When You Pay On Time,5344,WEAK,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::144_RUVOjfrIemY_Why_Your_Credit_Score_Drops_Even_When_You_Pay_On_Time.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""eb73f3f2a20f0e438d170cb582eedd85056d14f268c2bd7778f3fcc3da176d5a"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""RUVOjfrIemY"", ""Words"": ""3214""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The script has a clear household mystery, a recurring Thomas character and a useful organizing correction: a credit score is a model output from the file at a point in time, not a moral grade for paying bills. It moves from utilization timing through limits, inquiries, closure, credit mix, authorized users, model variation and errors, then pays off the mortgage setup and gives a practical checklist. The strongest advice is to inspect all reports, distinguish statement-close timing from the due date, avoid unnecessary applications and ask which model a lender uses. The main weakness is false precision. Universal utilization cliffs, point-loss ranges, removal timelines and model/population claims are presented as fixed even though FICO says the impact of one change depends on the complete file. Thomas is an unsourced composite, and the script stacks several separate hypothetical score movements into one causal story. The adversarial bureau-incentive section moves from documented dispute failures to an unsupported claim that opacity structurally benefits the system. The paper-only dispute and authorized-user 'clean history stays' advice are also too categorical.","[{""beat"": ""Credit Karma 738-to-716 cold open; paid-in-full paradox; Thomas mortgage setup and subscribe CTA"", ""character_range"": [0, 1740]}, {""beat"": ""Five FICO categories; utilization as reported-balance snapshot; statement date versus due date"", ""character_range"": [1740, 3530]}, {""beat"": ""Thomas work-trip example; issuer limit reduction; hard-versus-soft inquiry discussion"", ""character_range"": [3530, 5420]}, {""beat"": ""Closing a card raises utilization; closed-account aging; keep-or-downgrade advice"", ""character_range"": [5420, 7480]}, {""beat"": ""Paid-off installment loan and credit mix; authorized-user exposure and removal advice"", ""character_range"": [7480, 9260]}, {""beat"": ""Multiple FICO/VantageScore versions; mortgage-score mismatch; claimed rate-tier stakes"", ""character_range"": [9260, 11390]}, {""beat"": ""FTC error study; automated dispute handling; 2025 CFPB Experian and Equifax actions"", ""character_range"": [11390, 13750]}, {""beat"": ""Bureau/lender incentive critique; Thomas mortgage cost payoff; broader lending and insurance examples"", ""character_range"": [13750, 15910]}, {""beat"": ""Five-step checklist: reports, statement dates, old cards, lender model and authorized-user review; disclaimer and CTA"", ""character_range"": [15910, 18406]}]","[""SUPPORTED: CFPB guidance reviewed in January 2024 says paying a card on time can help scores but a high balance present when the score is calculated can still affect the result even if paid in full the next day. This supports the reported-balance timing mechanism, not the exact 22-point Thomas outcome."", ""SUPPORTED_WITH_CAVEAT: FICO's published factor categories are 35% payment history, 30% amounts owed, 15% history length, 10% new credit and 10% credit mix for the general FICO framework. FICO also says the percentages are broad population-level importance and a single factor's exact impact cannot be isolated without the full report. Calling 30% utilization a universal cliff and assigning 50-to-100-point losses is unsupported."", ""SUPPORTED_WITH_CAVEAT: FICO says a lender limit decrease or closure may raise utilization; the resulting score may fall, rise or stay the same because the entire file matters. Closed accounts can remain for years and continue helping history length. The script's utilization arithmetic is correct, but a fixed score response is not established."", ""SUPPORTED_WITH_CAVEAT: CFPB distinguishes hard inquiries after credit applications from soft inquiries such as prescreening and self-review. Hard inquiries can affect scores because models consider recency and frequency. FICO also groups qualifying mortgage, auto and student-loan rate-shopping inquiries by model-specific windows. The video's four-inquiry pattern, six-inquiry bankruptcy multiplier and 2.8-billion-mailer figure were not independently verified here."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: FICO says paying off a car loan can affect credit mix. It does not support a universal 10-to-25-point decline or the claim that a mortgage six weeks later necessarily costs a better rate. The effect is file- and model-dependent."", ""MISLEADING: The script says an authorized user's late mark remains for seven years after removal while the clean history stays. Reporting and removal practices vary; removing an authorized user commonly removes the account tradeline rather than selectively preserving only its positive history. No primary source reviewed supports the categorical 30-to-60-day or clean-history-stays claims."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: FICO documents multiple base and industry-specific score versions, including auto scores with a 250-to-900 range, and scores can differ by bureau and model. At the 2026-04-08 publication date, FHFA's 2022 approval of FICO 10T and VantageScore 4.0 existed, but the 2026-04-22 implementation announcement had not yet occurred. The transcript's universal statement that mortgage lenders currently use only FICO 2/4/5 and its exact 14- and 38-point model gaps were not fully verified."", ""OVERSTATED: The FTC's 1,001-participant study found one in four identified possible errors that might affect scores, one in five had a report modified after dispute, and about 5% had errors that could affect credit terms. Extrapolating the one-in-four figure into roughly 40 million Americans with meaningful errors 'right now' changes the study measure and ignores its 2012-era snapshot."", ""SUPPORTED_AS_ALLEGATION_OR_ORDER: CFPB sued Experian on 2025-01-07 alleging sham investigations and failure to convey relevant dispute information. CFPB ordered Equifax to pay a $15 million penalty on 2025-01-17 for improper investigations. These actions predate publication; allegations against Experian are not a final adjudication of every script claim."", ""UNSUPPORTED: The transcript's claim that credit bureaus and lenders profit from consumer confusion is an interpretation. Bureau data sales and risk-based pricing are real, but the reviewed sources do not establish a coordinated or structural causal incentive to keep scoring rules opaque."", ""CALCULATION_MISMATCH: For a $380,000 fully amortizing 30-year loan, monthly principal-and-interest payments are about $2,553.72 at 7.1% and $2,452.06 at 6.7%; the difference over 360 payments is about $36,599, not $31,200. Taxes, insurance, fees, points, prepayment and lender-specific pricing are omitted, so neither number is a universal borrower outcome."", ""PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED: CFPB says consumers can review reports without affecting scores and, since the pandemic, the nationwide reporting companies provide weekly access on request. The script's advice to use AnnualCreditReport.com is directionally sound. The assertion that a paper dispute creates legal standing that an online dispute does not is unsupported and should not be presented as legal advice.""]","You open Credit Karma on a Tuesday morning. Not because anything's wrong, just a quick check before work. You're expecting to see the 738 you had last month, maybe 740.","If you found this useful, subscribe. I do this kind of breakdown every week and if this video just saved you from making one of these mistakes, drop it in the comments. I genuinely like hearing when this stuff actually helps someone.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timing and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+R_E6OOnF9e4,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,15 Low Effort Boring Tasks That Make More Than Your Job,6609,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::140_R_E6OOnF9e4_15_Low_Effort_Boring_Tasks_That_Make_More_Than_Your_Job.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""70704b6d9146317a7029506a54fb72d8efdfad277b4a2529c3d4c6754441afe5"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""R_E6OOnF9e4"", ""Words"": ""3430""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:R_E6OOnF9e4"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:R_E6OOnF9e4"", ""audit-batches/NICK_154_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The repeated numbered structure is clear and unusually candid about several startup costs and operational burdens. The Jude/Mary frame gives the list a before/after story, and the close narrows fifteen ideas into three actions. The central flaw is category collapse: bank interest, securities, risky private loans, asset rental, active gig work and capital-intensive operating businesses are all called low-effort income streams. Exact earnings and survival figures are generally platform, vendor or anecdotal claims without citations. The tax framing incorrectly implies later income streams receive preferential treatment merely because they are additional. The final Mary arithmetic is not reproducible from the disclosed figures and timing, weakening the narrative payoff.","[""Jude-versus-Mary $58,000 salary and $1,240 side-income contrast"", ""Tax and inflation thesis; seven-income-stream claim"", ""Parking and storage rental"", ""Vending machines, ATMs and self-service car wash"", ""High-yield savings, Treasury bills and I bonds"", ""Dividend fund, REIT and real-estate lending"", ""Digital downloads, print on demand and stock media"", ""Focus groups/user testing and laundromat"", ""Mary $1,300 monthly recap and three-action close""]","[""MISLEADING: Employee payroll tax is generally 7.65% below applicable wage limits, but total take-home depends on filing status, deductions, benefits and state/local law. Rental income is not automatically taxed below wages, and dividends receive preferential federal rates only when qualified. Long-term capital-gain rates depend on taxable income and asset type."", ""UNVERIFIED: The claim that IRS data show millionaires average seven income streams was not tied to an IRS table and should not be presented as an IRS finding."", ""PARTIALLY SUPPORTED: FDIC's April 2026 national savings rate was 0.38%, close to the script's 0.39%. Online-account rates, T-bill yields and comparative multiples change over time; 'no trick, no catch' omits rate changes, account conditions and deposit-insurance limits."", ""DATE MISMATCH: TreasuryDirect supports a 4.03% composite I-bond rate for bonds issued November 2025 through April 2026, but the current May-October 2026 table is 4.26%. The script's number is plausible at publication, not timeless."", ""SUPPORTED_WITH_CAVEAT: SEC investor guidance confirms a qualifying REIT must distribute at least 90% of taxable income. That is not a guaranteed investor yield or return, and REIT prices, property values, financing and dividends carry risk."", ""OVERSTATED: Holding a REIT in a Roth IRA or 401(k) does not make every tax consideration 'disappear entirely'; account type, eligibility, withdrawal rules, fees and future law matter."", ""UNVERIFIED_VENDOR_OR_ANECDOTAL: Parking, storage, vending, ATM, car-wash, Groundfloor, Etsy, print-on-demand, stock-media, focus-group and laundromat earnings were not supported by primary evidence in the script. Platform averages and named successes do not establish typical net income after capital, vacancy, fees, taxes, insurance, defaults and labor."", ""UNSUPPORTED: A 94.8% five-year laundromat survival rate and comparison with restaurants were not tied to an authoritative cohort definition; the $40,000-$100,000 profit, 20-28% ROI and 10%-down acquisition example are not generally guaranteed."", ""ARITHMETIC/UNIT PROBLEM: Mary's disclosed $195 parking plus $190 storage plus $1,680 annual savings interest plus a $340 quarterly dividend equals about $638 per month before any Groundfloor amount. The script then says a $1,300 monthly total without disclosing enough principal or periodic cash flow to reproduce the gap."", ""PROMISE NOT DEMONSTRATED: 'Make more than your job' is not established for a typical viewer. The only stated annual business-profit range that can exceed $58,000 is active laundromat ownership, which the script admits is rarely absentee and requires capital and operational work.""]","Jude and Mary grew up in the same neighborhood, went to the same state school, graduated the same year. Both landed jobs in their mid-20s that pay right around $58,000.","I'm not a financial advisor and nothing here is financial advice. If this was useful, subscribe and I'll keep making these. See you in the next one.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timing and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+Rd40d0XZLpQ,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Shocking Money Stats of the Average Person,442496,STRONG,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::348_Rd40d0XZLpQ_Shocking_Money_Stats_of_the_Average_Person.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""ecb82f628146589bdc52cff62d7eaa13a4680ac4a74f2ed8f0eeca42000797be"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""Rd40d0XZLpQ"", ""Words"": ""3750""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:Rd40d0XZLpQ"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:Rd40d0XZLpQ"", ""audit-batches/NICK_155_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The script sustains one clear job: help viewers compare their finances with public benchmarks. It defines median, explains net worth, translates annual values into monthly stakes and closes with concrete behaviors. Its main weakness is evidence hygiene. Sources are named generically without table dates, and incompatible populations and measures are presented as one average person. Several figures reproduce exact 2021 or older tables even though newer official data were available by the September 2025 upload. The narrative repeatedly moves from descriptive population statistics to moral judgments about individual discipline, ignoring household composition, housing markets, health, caregiving, unemployment and selection into surveys or accounts. Arithmetic examples generally work, but the sourced inputs and inferences often do not.","[""Social-comparison anxiety hook and promise to locate the viewer financially"", ""Full-time weekly earnings and estimated after-tax take-home"", ""Retirement balances, account participation and four-percent withdrawal arithmetic"", ""Average Social Security benefit and program-funding caution"", ""Consumer-unit spending with housing, food and transportation breakdown"", ""Average FICO score and simplified credit-management advice"", ""Credit-card balance, interest arithmetic and debt-priority advice"", ""Household net worth by age and discipline interpretation"", ""Aggregate saving rate, major-purchase examples and retirement-saving recommendation"", ""Retirement age, longevity bridge and behavior-versus-goal conclusion""]","[""STALE/UNDERSTATED: BLS reports $1,204 median weekly earnings for full-time wage and salary workers in 2025, about $62,608 annualized. The script's just-over-$1,000/$52,000 value is not the most recent available benchmark and is not an all-worker average."", ""UNSUPPORTED RANGE: The claim that $52,000 gross becomes $35,000-$40,000 take-home depends on filing status, state, benefits, credits and deductions; no assumptions are provided."", ""DENOMINATOR PROBLEM: Federal Reserve SHED data show 28% of non-retired adults had no retirement savings in 2022. That does not mean one quarter of all Americans plans to rely entirely on Social Security. The $65,000 median and age balances are not tied to a specific official table and appear to mix account holders with broader populations."", ""ARITHMETIC SUPPORTED, RULE CONDITIONAL: Four percent of $88,000 is $3,520 per year, about $293 monthly. The four-percent rule is a planning heuristic with horizon, portfolio and inflation assumptions, not a guaranteed return; Social Security, pensions, work and other assets are excluded."", ""STALE: SSA reports a $1,975.34 average monthly retired-worker benefit in December 2024. The script's approximately $1,550 value is materially old by the September 2025 publication date. Benefits vary with covered earnings and claiming age."", ""EXACTLY REPRODUCED BUT STALE: $66,928 total spending, $22,624 housing, $8,289 food and $10,961 transportation are exact BLS 2021 consumer-unit means. They are not current 2025 figures, medians, family-only measures or a required after-tax break-even budget. The script also changes $5,577 to $5,500 in its closing savings example."", ""SUPPORTED: Experian reports an average FICO score of 715 in Q3 2024. The script's statement that some employers check credit scores is imprecise: employment screening generally uses credit reports, not consumer credit scores, and is subject to law and consent."", ""UNSUPPORTED/CONFLATED: Experian's Q3 2024 average credit-card balance was $6,730 per consumer with cards, not over $9,000 per household. Household debt, average balance, revolving balance and balance among indebted cardholders are different denominators. Saying most debt bought unnecessary goods is unsupported and ignores emergency or income-shock borrowing."", ""OUTDATED: The most recent SCF available at publication reported 2022 median family net worth of $192,900 overall and $39,000 for under-35 families. The script's approximately $122,000 and under-$14,000 values correspond to older survey/dollar vintages, yet are called most recent."", ""MEASURE MISCHARACTERIZED: BEA's personal saving rate is aggregate saving divided by aggregate disposable personal income, not the percent saved by the average person. September 2025 was 4.7%, not 6%; month-to-month revisions and volatility matter."", ""PARTLY SUPPORTED: Gallup's reported actual retirement age is around 61-62 in recent polls. This is a survey mean among retirees, not administrative evidence that most people choose to stop at 61. Full Social Security retirement age is 67 only for people born in 1960 or later; earlier cohorts have lower full retirement ages."", ""OVERGENERALIZED: SSA life-expectancy statistics around age 65 support roughly two further decades on average, but averages differ by sex and cohort and do not imply an individual 61-year-old will fund exactly 25 years or remain healthy for a specified span."", ""UNSUPPORTED CAUSAL/MORAL INFERENCE: Population averages do not establish that the average approach is failing mainly because of weak discipline, nor that saving slightly above an aggregate rate means a viewer is ahead. Income, household composition, benefits, debt cost, emergencies and regional prices materially affect outcomes.""]",Have you ever wondered if you're doing better or worse than everyone else when it comes to money?,"Because once you understand what most people are doing with their money, it becomes pretty clear how to do something different and get much better results. The choice is yours.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timing and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+RgaTGzeFcPQ,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Is Bitcoin going to 0? (What They Won't Tell You),6234,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::047_RgaTGzeFcPQ_Is_Bitcoin_going_to_0_What_They_Wont_Tell_You.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""531fd437e4ceebef6787d4325aa0a4084a7aa89c818930a46d35de1fc937d57a"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""RgaTGzeFcPQ"", ""Words"": ""3335""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RgaTGzeFcPQ"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RgaTGzeFcPQ"", ""audit-batches/NICK_156_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The script is a balanced narrative essay rather than a valuation model. It concedes network resilience and scarcity evidence, then distinguishes literal protocol failure from investor loss and time-horizon risk. Recurring Lindsay, Dale and Hollis stories make abstract mechanisms concrete, but they are illustrative composites, not documented cases. Several dated market figures are directionally supported, yet the script repeatedly converts vendor estimates or draft proposals into precise facts without naming the dataset, calculation date or uncertainty. It never estimates a defensible probability of zero and should not: Bitcoin has no cash-flow valuation anchor, market paths are uncertain, and survival does not guarantee a positive return. The close appropriately permits no position, but the thumbnail overstates the conclusion.","[""Contrarian zero-question hook and fictional Lindsay/Dale/Hollis characters"", ""Price drawdown versus prior Bitcoin bear markets"", ""Network-attack pathway dismissed using hash-rate and attack-cost claims"", ""ETF flows, miner sales and treasury-company liquidity as structural selling"", ""Bull case from failed obituaries, long-held supply, lost coins and issuance"", ""Holder losses, ownership estimates and typical position-size framing"", ""Reframe from literal zero to long-lived capital impairment"", ""Halving and fee-based security-budget problem"", ""Draft quantum-resistant address and migration proposals"", ""Three-question decision framework and non-position conclusion""]","[""SUPPORTED WITH DATE LIMIT: SEC filings describe Bitcoin above $126,000 in 2025 and Strategy reported $64,164 on 2026-07-24. Using $126,000 and $65,000 gives a 48.41% drawdown. Those endpoints do not prove one continuous October-to-recording series without the named price source and timestamp."", ""ARITHMETIC SUPPORTED: $67,600 to $15,700 is a 76.78% decline. The broader claim that every prior bear market lost 77%-93% depends on cycle definitions and an unnamed Fidelity analysis, so it is not independently established here."", ""UNVERIFIED PRECISION: Record hash rate may show continuing computation but does not prove economic security under every attack. The $10 billion hardware and $1.5 million-per-hour attack estimates omit hardware availability, topology, electricity prices, attack duration, defensive response and the distinction between hash control and guaranteed successful double spending."", ""PARTLY SUPPORTED: IBIT's SEC filing says U.S. spot Bitcoin products had their largest quarterly net outflows since launch in Q2 2026. It does not establish the script's exact June $4.0-$4.5 billion aggregate, BlackRock 79% share or claim that ETF flows explain 45% of weekly price movement; the script names no reproducible study or provider series."", ""PARTLY SUPPORTED, AGGREGATE UNVERIFIED: MARA disclosed about $1.5 billion of Q1 2026 Bitcoin sales to reduce debt, showing miner liquidity-driven selling. That filing does not prove the industry-wide 32,000-BTC total, comparison with all 2025, hashprice in the high $20s, or a universal $35 break-even. Those require a defined miner set and unit methodology."", ""MISSTATED AT PUBLICATION: Strategy's June 29 program allowed up to $1.25 billion of Bitcoin sales for its USD reserve, and its July 5 filing said the entire capacity remained available. Authorization was not a completed 20,000-coin sale. A later 10-Q reports actual July sales and roughly 843,775 coins at July 24, but that filing postdates the July 23 video and cannot validate publication-time sourcing."", ""DIRECTIONALLY SUPPORTED, CAUSAL OVERREACH: ETF redemptions, miner costs and treasury-company dividends can create selling independent of belief. Calling funds, miners and treasury companies the three largest structural holders and saying all became net sellers in the same six months requires consistent stock/flow definitions; Strategy remained a large net buyer during the first half of 2026."", ""UNVERIFIED VENDOR DATA: The 475-obituary count, 22 Peter Schiff entries, 14.8 million long-term-holder coins, 566 coins aging daily, 2.3-5 million permanently lost, 10.83 million underwater coins and 95% short-term-holder loss rate are not tied to primary records in the transcript. Wallet dormancy does not prove one owner, permanent loss or intent to hold."", ""ARITHMETIC CONDITIONAL: Investing $100 on each of 471 alleged obituary dates means $47,100 contributed. The asserted 1,043 BTC and roughly $67 million require the exact date-price convention, fees and a current price; the batch cannot reproduce them from the transcript alone. Calling it the most effective strategy ever is hyperbole, not an exhaustive investment comparison."", ""SUPPORTED SURVEY VALUE, WRONG LABEL: The Federal Reserve's probability-based survey of nearly 13,000 adults reports 10% used cryptocurrency for investment or transactions in 2025. It does not measure all-time ownership, and 10% cannot be directly compared with differently worded volunteer surveys as if each estimates the same population quantity."", ""ARITHMETIC SUPPORTED: A 43% loss requires a 75.44% gain to break even. At the stated $112,500 purchase midpoint and $65,000 current price, the loss is 42.22% and the required recovery is 73.08%; rounded narrative inputs explain the difference."", ""SUPPORTED PROTOCOL SCHEDULE, ECONOMICS UNCERTAIN: Bitcoin's subsidy halves every 210,000 blocks. Three halvings reduce 3.125 BTC to 0.390625 BTC, one eighth, around 2036 subject to block timing. If fees are only 0.61% of current miner revenue and total real revenue must stay constant, fees would need to grow about 143.6x after that subsidy decline. Neither the 0.61% input nor constant required security spending is established, and price, difficulty, energy cost, hardware efficiency and miner composition can change."", ""MISCHARACTERIZED TECHNICAL STATUS: BIP-360 is a draft proposal in the BIP repository, not merged activation in Bitcoin Core. BIP-361 is also draft and describes phased restrictions plus proposed rescue protocols; it is not an adopted decision to confiscate or permanently freeze a third of supply. Its own 34% exposed-public-key estimate is a proposal input, not proof those coins will be lost."", ""SPECULATION, NOT FACT: 'Almost certainly not' and any five-to-forty-year quantum timeline are judgments. No defensible probability of Bitcoin reaching zero, recovering within a holding period, or facing cryptographically relevant quantum attack can be calculated from the cited public material."", ""INVESTMENT-RISK LIMIT: Bitcoin can experience extreme volatility, liquidity shocks, custody loss, regulatory change, protocol/governance conflict, leverage cascades, dilution through wrappers or treasury vehicles and permanent capital loss without literally trading at zero. The audit is evidence review, not investment advice.""]",Nobody who says Bitcoin is going to zero actually believes Bitcoin is going to zero.,That is the whole test. Everything else is just somebody's story about why the next buyer is going to show up.,OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timed delivery and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+RmJKnJkUgxA,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,How Much Emergency Cash You Actually Need Saved,31648,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::329_RmJKnJkUgxA_How_Much_Emergency_Cash_You_Actually_Need_Saved.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""64c45c42a1d8468a96646ca6660e658e4d1874dc704afbf31cc120d29b7dac38"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""Words"": ""3206""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RmJKnJkUgxA"", ""audit-batches/NICK_157_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The script has a clear problem-solution progression and repeatedly relieves all-or-nothing saving anxiety. Concrete examples, rhetorical questions, recurring 'here's' pivots, numerical milestones and objection handling support retention, while the conclusion returns to the $2,000 anchor and immediate action. Its strongest substantive idea is that emergency liquidity should be sized to essential expenses and household risk, with a starter buffer before a larger income-shock reserve. Its weakest passages turn survey associations into equivalence, present old or unnamed survey figures as current national facts, treat credit availability and informal support as reliable substitutes for cash, and understate unemployment, insurance, market, transfer and product risks. The recommendations are planning heuristics, not universal rules or individualized financial advice.","[""3 a.m. car and appliance anxiety hook"", ""Challenge to the universal three-to-six-month rule"", ""$2,000 well-being claim and small-expense examples"", ""Survey statistics on $1,000 capacity, no fund and median savings"", ""Household-risk segmentation and essential-expense definition"", ""Starter-buffer and $25-per-week progress framing"", ""Reframing emergency-fund use as success followed by rebuilding"", ""Liquidity ladder using savings, money-market products and CDs"", ""Credit, sellable assets and family as backup resources"", ""Starter fund versus high-interest debt tradeoff"", ""Tiered small, medium and income-replacement funds"", ""Inflation, wages and purchasing-power warning"", ""Insurance, unemployment benefits and probability-based conclusion""]","[""SUPPORTED AS A RULE OF THUMB, NOT A UNIVERSAL RULE: Three to six months of expenses is common income-shock guidance, while CFPB says the amount depends on the person's situation. Three, six or nine months cannot be prescribed solely from family size, job type or salary without cash-flow, insurance, debt, benefits and risk information."", ""MISINTERPRETED ASSOCIATION: Vanguard estimated a 21% higher financial-well-being score for respondents with at least $2,000 of emergency savings versus 18% for $1 million-plus of assets in its regression. That does not mean $2,000 is economically 'as powerful' as $1 million, and observational association is not causal equivalence."", ""DATED PRIVATE SURVEY: Bankrate's December 2023 poll reported 44% could pay a $1,000 emergency from savings. It predates the September 2025 video by about 21 months and uses specific survey wording. Federal Reserve 2024 probability-survey results use different questions: 63% could cover $400 with cash or equivalent and 55% reported a three-month rainy-day fund."", ""UNREPRODUCED NATIONAL FIGURES: The transcript names no source for 42% having no emergency fund or a $500 median among people with emergency savings. These are not reproduced by the cited Federal Reserve measures and must not be combined across surveys as one population estimate."", ""ARITHMETIC SUPPORTED: $2,000 of essential monthly expenses times three months is $6,000. The expense base is a household decision and should include unavoidable obligations, timing and realistic reductions, not a universal list."", ""ARITHMETIC SUPPORTED: $25 per week for 52 weeks equals $1,300 before interest. The behavioral claim that this necessarily builds confidence is plausible but not established for every saver."", ""SUPPORTED CONCEPT: Using an emergency fund for a genuine shock is not fund failure; replenishment afterward is part of the plan. Whether a cost is unexpected, necessary and urgent is household-specific."", ""INCORRECT GENERALIZATION: Credit cards do not guarantee 'at least 30 days' of free financing. Statements generally must provide at least 21 days to the due date, but grace periods are not required and interest avoidance depends on product terms, transaction type, prior balance and full timely payment. Credit limits can also be reduced or unavailable during stress."", ""PRODUCT DISTINCTION REQUIRED: High-yield savings accounts and bank money-market deposit accounts can be insured deposits within applicable limits; money-market mutual funds are securities, are not FDIC-insured and can lose value. CDs may impose early-withdrawal restrictions or penalties. Transfer and settlement timing should be tested before treating any tier as emergency cash."", ""CONDITIONAL TRADEOFF: At the stated simple annual rates, 20% debt minus 1% savings is a 19-percentage-point spread, not proof that every dollar beyond $500-$1,000 should repay debt. Minimum liquidity, employer match, delinquency risk, tax, promotional rates, credit access and likely shock size can change sequencing."", ""UNVERIFIED AVERAGE: The transcript provides no reproducible source or definition for an 'average emergency' below $2,000. Spending shocks and income shocks differ in frequency, duration and severity; an average does not size tail-risk protection for a household."", ""SUPPORTED DIRECTION, UNSUPPORTED EXAMPLES: Inflation reduces the purchasing power of fixed cash and justifies periodic recalculation. The specific claims that $500 repairs became $1,000, $100 medical bills became $300 and $200 groceries became $300 are anecdotes without a defined item, geography or interval; the blanket claim that wages failed to keep pace for most people is also unsourced."", ""MATERIAL RISK OMISSION: Unemployment insurance is partial and eligibility/duration vary. Health insurance can leave premiums, deductibles, copays, coinsurance, noncovered and out-of-network costs. Homeowners policies have deductibles, exclusions and claim timing. These protections reduce some risks but do not make months-long cash needs rare for every household."", ""RECOMMENDATION BOUNDARY: A tiered fund and balance between debt repayment, cash and investment opportunity can be reasonable, but the correct allocation depends on liquidity needs and risk tolerance. No universal optimal emergency-fund amount, debt sequence or investment comparison is established by the public evidence.""]",Picture this. You're lying in bed at 3:00 in the morning staring at the ceiling wondering if you could survive financially if your car decided to transform into an expensive pile of metal tomorrow.,Stop letting perfect be the enemy of good and start building your safety net today. Your future self will thank you when life inevitably decides to get expensive at the worst possible moment.,OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timed delivery and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+S0xwBH_fAn0,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Why $100K Doesn’t Feel Rich Anymore,13180,WEAK,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::290_S0xwBH_fAn0_Why_100K_Doesnt_Feel_Rich_Anymore.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""63fb4984aeaf2b7cc3f80a6747cbbf1f28b17f0a141f7ea68a6732c9c28907b4"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""S0xwBH_fAn0"", ""Words"": ""3240""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:S0xwBH_fAn0"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:S0xwBH_fAn0"", ""audit-batches/NICK_158_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The narrative progresses from personal disappointment to structural costs and then to controllable spending psychology. Direct questions, budget line items, rising-number sequences, geographic contrasts and repeated status reversals sustain attention. The close usefully distinguishes income from spending, but several statistics are unnamed, selected-population estimates or incorrect generalizations. Combining taxes, inflation, housing, debt and psychology explains possible pressure; it does not prove one causal reason a particular earner feels poor.","[""Milestone-without-relief empathy hook"", ""Paycheck-to-paycheck and tax explanation"", ""Major-city household budget"", ""Inflation and lost purchasing power"", ""Lifestyle inflation and social comparison"", ""Housing prices, required income and buyer age"", ""Student and professional education debt"", ""Geographic purchasing-power differences"", ""Survey comfort and rich-income anchors"", ""Relative-spending psychology conclusion""]","[""UNVERIFIED PRIVATE-SURVEY GENERALIZATION: The claim that nearly half of $100,000 earners live paycheck to paycheck is not tied to a named methodology and 'paycheck to paycheck' has no single official definition."", ""INCORRECT AS A UNIVERSAL TAX CLAIM: For a 2025 single filer with wages only and the standard deduction, federal income tax plus employee FICA is about $21,264.50, or 21.26% of $100,000, before state/local tax. Location, filing status, deductions and benefits change take-home pay; government does not universally take 40%."", ""ILLUSTRATIVE, NOT REPRESENTATIVE: The city budget can be arithmetically plausible but is not sourced to a defined household, location, housing tenure or benefit package and cannot represent all six-figure earners."", ""NOT REPRODUCED BY CPI-U: November 2015 $100,000 equals about $136,567 in November 2025 dollars; November 2005 $100,000 equals about $164,029. The script's $132,000 and $175,000 may use different dates or measures but none is named. The November 2025 endpoint also postdates upload."", ""SUPPORTED DIRECTION, NOT UNIVERSAL MAGNITUDE: Lifestyle creep and comparison can raise perceived needs, but the script supplies no causal study establishing its prevalence or effect size among $100,000 earners."", ""MIXED HOUSING METRICS: A national median above $415,000 depends on whether the measure covers new homes, existing homes, sale period or listing data. City examples and an income-needed estimate require interest-rate, down-payment, tax, insurance, debt and underwriting assumptions."", ""SUPPORTED WITH POPULATION LIMIT: NAR reported median age 40 for successful first-time primary-residence buyers in its 2025 survey, not for all aspiring buyers. Its adjusted response rate was 3.5%."", ""DEBT DENOMINATORS MATTER: Average balances among all people, borrowers, graduates with debt and specific professional-school cohorts are not interchangeable. The transcript's $40,000, $80,000, $313,000 and $130,000 figures lack consistent named populations and vintages."", ""GEOGRAPHY CLAIM SUPPORTED IN PRINCIPLE: Nominal $100,000 purchasing power differs with housing, tax and service costs, but selected metros do not establish an optimal relocation or universal lifestyle outcome."", ""UNVERIFIED PERCEPTION ANCHOR: The $520,000 income needed to feel rich and income-scaled comfort targets are private survey perceptions, not objective wealth requirements or individualized planning rules."", ""RECOMMENDATION BOUNDARY: Resisting automatic lifestyle expansion, tracking spending, managing debt and limiting social comparison may help. They are behavioral suggestions, not proof that structural costs or income inadequacy are imaginary.""]",Have you ever hit a major financial milestone and then felt absolutely nothing? Like you thought reaching six figures was going to be this transformative moment where you'd finally feel wealthy and instead you're still checking your bank account with the same anxiety you had when you were making half that.,"The people who feel rich are the ones who consciously resist lifestyle inflation, who understand their geographic advantage or disadvantage, who've paid off their debts or are managing them strategically, who aren't comparing themselves to everyone on social media who understand what their money is doing and where it's going. that they feel rich because they're rich relative to their spending, not relative to their income. And that distinction changes everything about how they experience their financial life.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timed delivery and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+SJLyXAGbBLk,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,A Simple Japanese Money Trick to Become 35% Richer,6817,WEAK,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::182_SJLyXAGbBLk_A_Simple_Japanese_Money_Trick_to_Become_35_Richer.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""9eb919f3b238af3e18a3736c98d1f8f12343b15088da9c86d06e1551b18601c9"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""SJLyXAGbBLk"", ""Words"": ""3458""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:SJLyXAGbBLk"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:SJLyXAGbBLk"", ""audit-batches/NICK_159_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The narrative opens with novelty and a relatable anxiety, earns credibility by challenging its own headline, then moves from history to behavioral mechanisms and a usable routine. Callbacks to Hani's memory slogan and concrete purchase examples create cohesion. The practical system is clear, but evidence from note-taking, memory generation and workplace goal accountability is repeatedly transferred to budgeting outcomes without a direct Kakeibo trial. The script also contrasts finance-app usage with aggregate debt as though contemporaneous movement demonstrated failure; those series do not identify causation.","[""1904 system and late-night banking-anxiety hook"", ""Immediate disclaimer of the 35% headline"", ""Motoko Hani origin story"", ""Budget-app retention and debt contrast"", ""Pain-of-paying and manual-friction mechanism"", ""Handwriting, generation-effect and goal-writing evidence"", ""Monthly setup and four spending categories"", ""Four reflection questions and 24-hour pause"", ""Restriction versus attention philosophy"", ""Step-by-step implementation"", ""Invisible-spending payoff and historical callback""]","[""CORRECTLY DISCLAIMED: The 35% figure is not presented as a research result. No controlled Kakeibo savings study or defined denominator is supplied, so it cannot support a guaranteed or typical wealth increase."", ""PARTLY SUPPORTED HISTORY: Japan's National Diet Library supports Hani as Japan's first woman journalist and the 1903 launch of Katei no Tomo. The transcript's claim that she held that status 'in 1904' is imprecise because she joined Hochi-sha in 1897. The 1904 ledger origin is widely documented, but exact original questions, categories, slogan continuity and unchanged bookstore form were not verified from a primary 1904 artifact."", ""CATEGORY MISMATCH: A commercial analytics source reports roughly 9% day-30 retention for the broad finance-app category, not specifically budgeting apps and not the script's full 4-9% range. The 84% annual abandonment figure was not reproduced from a named cohort."", ""NON-CAUSAL DEBT COMPARISON: New York Fed data can measure household and card balances, but rising aggregate debt does not show that budgeting apps failed. Latest releases relative to the March 2026 upload, definitions and release dates must be stated; later Q1/Q2 2026 values cannot be silently treated as publication-era evidence."", ""UNREPRODUCED POPULATION CLAIMS: The 65% paycheck-to-paycheck and 42% no-savings figures lack named surveys, populations, dates and definitions in the script."", ""SUPPORTED EXPERIMENT, OVEREXTENDED APPLICATION: A 2024 36-student EEG study found more elaborate connectivity during stylus handwriting than single-finger typing. It did not test budgets, saving or financial behavior, so it cannot establish that a paper ledger outperforms an app."", ""REPLICATION LIMIT: The original longhand note-taking result is not settled. Direct replications and mini meta-analyses found no reliable immediate-learning advantage, so 'consistently outperformed' is too categorical."", ""SUPPORTED MEMORY EFFECT, NOT BUDGET EFFECT: The generation-effect meta-analysis covered 86 studies and estimated about 0.40 standard deviations for generated versus read material, with substantial moderator variation. It did not establish that manually recording purchases reduces repeat spending."", ""CONFOUNDED GOAL COMPARISON: Dominican University's summary says 267 people were recruited, but the >70% group sent weekly updates to a friend while the 35% group kept goals private without writing. The comparison combines writing, action commitments and accountability; it does not show that writing alone doubled achievement."", ""PRACTICAL GUIDANCE, NOT GUARANTEE: Recording spending, setting an explicit savings target and pausing before optional purchases can support awareness. The proposed 5%/10% goals, four-way weekly division and 24-hour rule are heuristics, not universally validated outcomes or individualized financial advice.""]","In 1904, Japan's first female journalist invented a budgeting system that people still buy unchanged in bookstores today. No app, no algorithm, just paper, a pen, and four questions. It's called Kakeibo.","You need a few minutes a day, a savings goal you actually write down, and the habit of asking yourself once a month how you can improve. That's the whole thing. If this video made something click, subscribe and hit the like button. It tells the algorithm this is worth spreading, and if you want to see what happens when you take those savings and actually put them to work, the video on compound interest is right here. I'll see you there.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timed delivery and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+SLFcypMv6I0,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,"SpaceX IPO Just went public, Don't Buy Until You See This!",8416,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::083_SLFcypMv6I0_SpaceX_IPO_Just_went_public_Dont_Buy_Until_You_See_This.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""04838759d4a9f8d4ad130cdbda71ec1ac6874edf6f6199cd8ac12c6416dbc1c1"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""SLFcypMv6I0"", ""Words"": ""3246""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:SLFcypMv6I0"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:SLFcypMv6I0"", ""audit-batches/NICK_160_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The script opens with a concrete FOMO vignette, states its price-versus-quality thesis early, then moves through valuation, float, governance, historical base rates and counterexamples before returning to Jerome. It is unusually balanced for a warning video because it names successful IPO counterexamples and concedes that SpaceX may compound for decades. The central valuation arithmetic is reproducible, but several statements overstate what was known on publication day: the opening ticker is misnamed SPCE rather than SPCX; the actual debut opened at $150, not around $170-$200; Nasdaq-100 inclusion was not announced until June 26; and index trackers buy because they track an index, not because every index fund is universally forced to own every qualifying company. The script also treats a broad IPO base rate and later share unlocks as if they predict SpaceX's path, which they do not.","[""Jerome buys the post-IPO surge"", ""Great-company versus sane-price thesis"", ""Offering size and record framing"", ""Revenue, loss and price-to-sales valuation"", ""Bull case and execution dependencies"", ""Small float and anticipated index demand"", ""First-day underpricing transfer"", ""Dual-class governance and key-person risk"", ""IPO base-rate and named losers"", ""ARM, Reddit and Tesla counterexamples"", ""Great company versus good stock callback"", ""Lock-up supply and patience"", ""Alternative entry methods and closing discipline""]","[""IDENTITY ERROR: The opening says ticker SPCE, which is Virgin Galactic. SpaceX listed as SPCX. This is material because the story purports to quote live app prices for the target security."", ""PRICE SEQUENCE OVERSTATED: Nasdaq says SPCX opened at $150 on June 12, 11% above the $135 offer. It later traded above $200 during the first week, so a $206 purchase was possible, but not at the initial opening described. The $206/$135 premium is 52.59%, reasonably rounded to 50%."", ""OFFERING MATH SUPPORTED WITH GROSS/NET DISTINCTION: 555,555,555 shares at $135 equals about $75.0 billion gross. After the full 15% over-allotment, 638,888,888 shares imply $86.25 billion gross; the later 10-Q reports $85.675 billion net after $575 million of commissions and costs. Calling $85.7 billion the total is accurate only as net proceeds, not gross offering size."", ""VALUATION MULTIPLE REPRODUCES: $1.77 trillion divided by 2025 revenue of $18.674 billion is about 94.79x sales. The filing supports roughly $4.94 billion GAAP net loss and a roughly $41.3 billion accumulated deficit, but those consolidated figures include acquired xAI/X history and should not be presented as pure legacy rocket-company economics."", ""STARLINK CLAIM PARTLY SUPPORTED: The S-1 reports about 10.3 million subscribers at March 31, 2026 and profitable Connectivity segment operations. 'Mostly buying Starlink' and software-like margins are valuation interpretations; the listed company also contains Space and AI segments and their assets, liabilities and growth options."", ""FLOAT APPROXIMATION: The base 555.6 million offering was about 4.4% of approximately 12.52 billion post-conversion shares; the fully exercised 638.9 million offering was about 4.9%. Saying only 4% floated is a reasonable base-offering approximation but excludes the greenshoe."", ""INDEX TIMING ERROR: At the June 17 upload, accelerated Nasdaq-100 inclusion was anticipated, not completed or announced. Nasdaq announced on June 26 that SPCX would join July 7. The later event cannot validate the script's present-tense statement that a low-cost index fund 'now owns' SpaceX."", ""INDEX MECHANISM OVERSIMPLIFIED: Funds contractually tracking the Nasdaq-100 generally rebalance to the constituent change, but SpaceX did not automatically qualify for every major index and all index funds are not compelled to buy it. Nasdaq later found additions often weakened after inclusion, which supports uncertainty rather than a guaranteed price prop."", ""UNDERPRICING CALCULATION PLAUSIBLE, FRAMING IMPRECISE: Using the $160.95 first-day close and 638.9 million issued shares gives about $16.58 billion of first-day money left on the table, close to $17 billion. This is issuer value transferred to allocated buyers; it is not simply the difference between what all insiders paid and what retail buyers paid."", ""GOVERNANCE SUPPORTED: The prospectus establishes one vote for Class A and ten votes for Class B and disclosed roughly 85% Musk voting control before dilution from the offering. Public investors have limited voting influence. Claims that no shareholder vote can stop any action overstate legal, board, fiduciary, regulatory and class-specific constraints."", ""LOCK-UP DIRECTIONALLY SUPPORTED, OUTCOME NOT GUARANTEED: The offering document provides staggered releases beginning after Q2 2026 earnings for portions of non-Musk holdings and longer restrictions for Musk and selected insiders. Eligible supply can rise; a price decline is possible, not mechanically required."", ""BASE-RATE BOUNDARY: Jay Ritter's research archive supports long-run IPO underperformance as a historical research topic and documents substantial underpricing. It does not establish that every hyped, low-float IPO underperforms or that SpaceX will follow Facebook, Rivian, Coinbase or Robinhood. ARM, Reddit and Tesla counterexamples correctly expose heterogeneity."", ""INVESTMENT GUIDANCE REMAINS CONDITIONAL: Waiting, dollar-cost averaging and diversified index exposure can reduce timing or concentration risk, but none guarantees a better entry. Suitability depends on valuation assumptions, horizon, risk capacity, taxes and portfolio concentration.""]","Jerome refreshes the app for the fourth time in 90 seconds. Friday morning, the ticker finally goes live and there it is, SPCE climbing 170, 180, 200. He's waited 18 months for this, the chance to own a piece of the company that lands rockets like it's parallel parking.","There is no trophy for buying first. There's no medal for catching the top of the pop. The prize in investing doesn't go to the person who's earliest or loudest. It goes to the person who's still standing, still calm, still buying good things at fair prices long after everyone else has moved on to the next shiny rocket. Be that person, watch, wait, run the numbers, and let the confetti settle before you decide it's a party worth paying for. If this helped you think a little more clearly, the community links down below, and I'll see you in the next one.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timed delivery and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
+SU-3ZcgmCJ4,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Are You In The Top 50  Of Americans Financially (Surprising Data),18293,WEAK,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::336_SU-3ZcgmCJ4_Are_You_In_The_Top_50_Of_Americans_Financially_Surprising_Data.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""85c30461e0fcd9521662c764747fd6e9b812834c1545e8537b7a2a026a389b95"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""SU-3ZcgmCJ4"", ""Words"": ""3063""}",FULL_STORED_TEXT,TEXT_AVAILABLE,"[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:SU-3ZcgmCJ4"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:SU-3ZcgmCJ4"", ""audit-batches/NICK_161_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","The script opens with identity anxiety, promises an exact relative rank, then moves metric by metric through income, wealth, liquidity, debt, housing, retirement and saving. Rhetorical questions, 'shocking' reversals, concrete household vignettes and repeated relief-versus-panic framing maintain an information gap. The conclusion pivots usefully from current rank to trajectory, but the narrative never reconciles its measures into one rank and treats aggregates as individual norms. Several auto-caption values are visibly corrupted, while other errors remain substantive even if audio were perfect. The advice to track spending and build buffers is general; the claimed statistical precision is not.","[""Social-comparison hook and subscribe prompt"", ""National income median and middle-class band"", ""Net-worth definition and national median"", ""Age-specific net-worth benchmarks"", ""Three-month emergency resources"", ""Aggregate debt and debt-service burden"", ""Credit-card and medical-debt warnings"", ""Housing burden and $250,000-home claim"", ""Massachusetts-versus-Mississippi geography"", ""Defined-contribution balances"", ""Retirement targets and Social Security"", ""Personal-saving-rate claim"", ""Geographic arbitrage anecdotes"", ""Savings-rate trajectory and closing motivation""]","[""INCOME SUPPORTED, CLASS BAND NOT OFFICIAL: Census reported 2024 real median household income of $83,730 before the September 23 publication. Half above and half below is correct for that distribution. The stated $53,740-$161,220 middle-class band does not equal two-thirds to double $83,730, which is $55,820-$167,460, and 'middle class' is not an official Census cutoff."", ""NET-WORTH DATE AND UNIT ERROR: The Federal Reserve SCF reports 2022 median family net worth of $192,900, not a 2023 household estimate of $192,084. Its comparable 2019 value in 2022 dollars was $141,100, not $121,411. Cross-source inflation bases may explain some variants, but the script does not disclose them."", ""AGE MEDIANS MATERIALLY WRONG: The SCF age medians are $39,000 under 35, $135,600 ages 35-44, $247,200 ages 45-54, $364,500 ages 55-64 and $409,900 ages 65-74. Only the first figure roughly matches. The canonical caption's '212,166,000' is impossible and original audio was not inspected."", ""EMERGENCY SPLIT SUPPORTED: The 2024 SHED supports 55% with three months of rainy-day savings, another 15% able to cover by borrowing, asset sales or other savings, and 30% unable by any means. These are adults' self-reports, not audited household balance sheets."", ""DEBT DENOMINATOR FAILURE: The New York Fed supports $18.388 trillion total household debt in Q2 2025 and $1.209 trillion credit-card balances. It does not support $15,056 per household. Even a crude division by about 132 million households produces roughly $139,000, and mean aggregate debt is not typical household debt. The later sentence says over $100,000, contradicting the caption's $15,056."", ""DEBT-SERVICE INTERPRETATION OVERSTATED: A roughly 11.1% aggregate debt-service ratio is plausible, but it is required payments divided by aggregate disposable personal income. It does not show every indebted household is managing better, and methodological changes complicate long comparisons."", ""CREDIT-CARD STORY MORALIZES WITHOUT EVIDENCE: Aggregate revolving balances and high rates do not establish that balances mainly represent forgotten dinners, unused clothes or impulse purchases. Households also use cards for necessities and shocks. The transcript supplies no primary source for $10,815 among revolving households."", ""MEDICAL-DEBT CLAIM UNREPRODUCED: The $220 billion and 40% claims are not supported by the cited federal sources located for this review. SHED reports 15%-18% of adults with outstanding medical debt in recent years; broader definitions can differ, so source, unit and definition are required."", ""HOUSING CLAIMS FALSELY GENERALIZED: Census reports 46.3% of renters cost-burdened and 23.9% severely burdened in 2023, while homeowners have lower rates. This does not support 76% of all Americans over 31% or more than half over 50%. The 30% benchmark is a burden definition, not an individual affordability law."", ""STATE FIGURES CORRUPTED OR WRONG: Official 2023 ACS medians were $99,858 in Massachusetts and $54,203 in Mississippi. The canonical caption renders $16,500 and $441,966, then later $16,000 and $44,900. Original audio is unavailable, so exact spoken values cannot be certified; the transcript still cannot support the claimed arithmetic."", ""HOME-AFFORDABILITY CLAIM BLOCKED: No named source or disclosed interest rate, down payment, taxes, insurance, debts or underwriting method is given for 66.6 million households unable to afford a $250,000 home. Affordability cannot be inferred from price alone."", ""401(K) POPULATION BOUNDARY: Vanguard supports a $148,153 average and $38,176 median among nearly five million participants in its defined-contribution plans. This is not all Americans and excludes other plans and assets. Current-plan balances are partial preparedness measures; the transcript's 20s and 50s age examples do not match Vanguard's table."", ""RETIREMENT-SAVINGS CLAIM REVERSED/UNSUPPORTED: Federal Reserve evidence says 67% of adults had a tax-preferred retirement account or pension in 2024. The claim that 54% had no retirement savings is not supported and may invert a statistic about ownership or plan type."", ""SOCIAL SECURITY ERROR: SSA estimated $1,976 monthly for all retired workers in January 2025, not $1,550. Benefit adequacy depends on household composition, claiming age, other income and local costs."", ""SAVING-RATE CATEGORY ERROR: BEA's November 2024 vintage showed 4.4%, while the later December release showed 3.8%. The personal saving rate is aggregate personal saving divided by aggregate disposable income, not the percentage saved by the average person. Saving 10% does not by itself prove top-half financial position."", ""CAUSAL CLAIMS UNSOURCED: The assertions that consistent 10% savers climb wealth rankings and lifestyle inflators slide down are plausible hypotheses, not results established by the cited cross-sectional figures. Income, returns, inheritance, housing, health, family size, taxes and shocks also affect rank.""]","Are you secretly crushing it financially without even realizing it? Or are you part of the struggling masses who think everyone else has their money figured out? The answer might shock you because most Americans have absolutely no clue where they actually stand compared to their neighbors, co-workers, and that guy on Instagram who somehow affords a Tesla on a barista salary.","So, there you have it. Whether you're crushing it financially or barely keeping your head above water, at least now you know exactly where you stand compared to everyone else. Use these numbers as motivation, not as an excuse to panic or get complacent. Your financial future depends on what you do next, not where you started.",OPENING_WORDS_ONLY,"FULL_AVAILABLE_STORED_TEXT_READ_HASH_VERIFIED; auto-generated captions; original audio, timed delivery and editing unverified",,False,,,,,
 S_8SLGjLrBM,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Who Can Actually Afford a $750K House in 2026? (SHOCKING Math),6828,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,NOT_STARTED,"{""Caption_type"": ""auto_generated"", ""Dataset"": ""DS-000022"", ""Duplicate_of"": """", ""Evidence_locator"": ""data/extracted/channel-data/DS-000022/packages/nick-invests/NickInvests_Transcripts_363_Videos.zip::123_S_8SLGjLrBM_Who_Can_Actually_Afford_a_750K_House_in_2026_SHOCKING_Math.txt"", ""Human_verified"": ""NO"", ""Status"": ""TEXT_AVAILABLE"", ""Text_SHA256"": ""b4d5f9bc88b796b0372425d31dc946eb6048c886b2d83aebac1adddf9417d611"", ""Timing"": ""PLAINTEXT_NO_TIMED_SEGMENTS_EXTRACTED"", ""Video_ID"": ""S_8SLGjLrBM"", ""Words"": ""3387""}",NOT_REVIEWED,TEXT_AVAILABLE,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,